  m_Name: StereoscopicCamera
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  exhibitName: Stereoscopic Camera
  description: "Stereoscopic Camera by Heinrich Ernemann \nGermany, Late 19th \u2013
    Early 20th Century \nThis stereoscopic camera, produced by the Ernemann-Werke
    company in Dresden, represents an important stage in the evolution of early photographic
    technology. Featuring two precisely aligned lenses, the instrument was designed
    to capture simultaneous images from slightly different angles, mirroring human
    binocular vision. When viewed through a stereoscope, these paired photographs
    created a striking three-dimensional effect, offering audiences one of the earliest
    forms of immersive visual experience. \nHeinrich Ernemann\u2014an influential
    German optical engineer\u2014was renowned for his precision craftsmanship and
    innovations in shutter and lens design. Cameras of this type were widely used
    for scientific documentation, travel photography, educational collections, and
    popular entertainment, reflecting the era\u2019s fascination with realism and
    depth perception. \nConstructed with durable metal fittings and a compact folding
    housing, this camera showcases the engineering sophistication of pre-war German
    optical manufacturing. Today, it stands as a key artifact in the history of photography,
    illustrating the technological imagination that paved the way for modern 3D imaging
    and virtual visualization methods. "
  images: []
